=== L2 Circuit Symbols, Voltage and Current Rules ===
id: 2d4d4195-a124-4ac7-b735-5a64b7ad7e82
slug: circuit-symbols-voltage-and-current-rules
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:26:37.357058+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 39

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Standard circuit symbols, series and parallel rules, and how to use test equipment.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Why Symbols and Rules Matter</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Before any circuit can be built or analysed, it must be drawn correctly. Examiners award marks for circuits using the correct standard symbols &mdash; a battery drawn as a single line scores no marks even if the rest of the circuit is perfect. Equally, getting the voltage and current rules wrong can cascade errors through every subsequent calculation. This lesson gives you the foundations everything else builds on.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Quantities, Units and Symbols</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Three quantities appear in almost every electronics calculation. Confusing their units is the most commonly penalised error in exams.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="Cover this table and write down the quantity, symbol and unit for voltage, current and resistance from memory. Check your spelling of ohm and ampere.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p><strong>Quantity &mdash; Symbol &mdash; Unit &mdash; Unit symbol:</strong><br>
  Voltage &mdash; V &mdash; volt &mdash; V<br>
  Current &mdash; I &mdash; ampere &mdash; A<br>
  Resistance &mdash; R &mdash; ohm &mdash; &Omega;</p>
</div>

<p data-narration-id="n6">Always include the unit symbol in your answer. Writing &ldquo;5&rdquo; when the answer is &ldquo;5 V&rdquo; will cost you a mark. Voltage is measured in <strong>volts (V)</strong>, current in <strong>amperes (A)</strong> and resistance in <strong>ohms (&Omega;)</strong>. These three are linked by Ohm&rsquo;s law, covered in the next lesson.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Standard Circuit Symbols</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Circuit diagrams use internationally agreed symbols. You are expected to know and draw the symbols for these components: <dfn class="term" data-def="A source of electrical energy, represented in a circuit diagram as a pair of parallel lines of unequal length (long line = positive terminal).">cell</dfn>, battery (multiple cells), resistor (rectangle), variable resistor (rectangle with an arrow), lamp, LED (arrow pointing away from the triangle), diode (triangle with a bar), capacitor (two parallel lines), switch, relay, fuse, voltmeter (circle with V), ammeter (circle with A), LDR (resistor with arrows pointing inward), thermistor (resistor with a T or slanted line), npn transistor, MOSFET, operational amplifier (triangle with + and &minus; inputs), AND gate, OR gate, NOT gate, NAND gate and NOR gate.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">Use only these standard symbols in exam circuit drawings. A rough sketch of a real component &mdash; or any invented symbol &mdash; scores zero. The specification appendix shows the exact forms required.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Series Circuits</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">In a <dfn class="term" data-def="A circuit in which components are connected end-to-end so that the same current flows through every component.">series circuit</dfn>, all components sit on a single loop. Two key rules apply:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n12"><strong>Current is the same everywhere.</strong> If 20 mA flows through one resistor in a series chain, 20 mA flows through every other component in that chain.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n13"><strong>Voltages add up.</strong> The sum of the voltages across each component equals the supply voltage: \(V_S = V_1 + V_2 + V_3\). This is a consequence of Kirchhoff&rsquo;s Voltage Law.</li>
</ul>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n14" data-revision-tip="Close this and complete these two sentences: In a series circuit, current is _____ everywhere; voltages _____. Write the equation linking supply voltage to component voltages.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Series circuit rules: current is the <strong>same</strong> at every point; voltages across each component <strong>add up</strong> to the supply voltage: \(V_S = V_1 + V_2 + \ldots\)</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">Parallel Circuits</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16">In a <dfn class="term" data-def="A circuit in which components are connected across the same two nodes so that each component has the same voltage across it.">parallel circuit</dfn>, each branch connects directly between the same two points. Two rules apply:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n17"><strong>Voltage is the same across every branch.</strong> A 9 V supply means 9 V appears across every parallel branch.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n18"><strong>Currents add up at junctions.</strong> The total current from the supply equals the sum of the currents in all parallel branches: \(I_T = I_1 + I_2 + I_3\). This is Kirchhoff&rsquo;s Current Law.</li>
</ul>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Worked Example: Series and Parallel Voltage and Current</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n19"><strong>Series example:</strong> A 9 V supply drives two resistors in series. Voltmeter across R1 reads 4 V. What is the voltage across R2?</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n20">\(V_S = V_1 + V_2\) &rArr; \(9 = 4 + V_2\) &rArr; \(V_2 = 5 \text{ V}\)</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n21"><strong>Parallel example:</strong> Two branches carry 30 mA and 20 mA. What current does the supply deliver?</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n22">\(I_T = I_1 + I_2 = 30 + 20 = 50 \text{ mA}\)</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n23">Test Equipment</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n24">You need to know how to connect and use four instruments:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n25"><strong>Voltmeter &mdash;</strong> always connected in <em>parallel</em> with the component being measured. Has very high input resistance so it draws negligible current.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n26"><strong>Ammeter &mdash;</strong> always connected in <em>series</em> in the branch being measured. Has very low resistance so it introduces minimal voltage drop.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n27"><strong>Logic probe &mdash;</strong> detects HIGH or LOW logic levels in digital circuits. It lights a different LED for logic 1 and logic 0, useful for testing gate outputs quickly.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n28"><strong>Oscilloscope &mdash;</strong> displays a graph of voltage against time. The horizontal axis is time (controlled by the time-base setting, in ms/div or &mu;s/div); the vertical axis is voltage (controlled by the Y-gain, in V/div or mV/div). Used to measure waveform amplitude, period and frequency.</li>
</ul>
<p data-narration-id="n29">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A multi-function test instrument that can measure voltage, current or resistance depending on the mode selected.">multimeter</dfn> can measure voltage, current or resistance. Selecting the wrong mode &mdash; for example, connecting it in parallel on the current setting &mdash; can damage the instrument because it presents almost zero resistance across the supply.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Signal Generator Waveforms and the Oscilloscope</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n30">A signal generator produces test waveforms. Three types appear in the specification:</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n31"><strong>Sine wave &mdash;</strong> a smoothly varying AC signal. The oscilloscope shows a regular S-curve. Amplitude is the peak height above zero, measured in volts from the centre line to the peak (NOT peak-to-peak).</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n32"><strong>Square wave &mdash;</strong> switches instantly between HIGH and LOW. Used to test digital circuits. Each half-period at HIGH and LOW should be equal for a 50% duty cycle.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n33"><strong>Triangular wave &mdash;</strong> rises and falls linearly at a constant rate. Used for testing linearity of circuits.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n34">Reading an oscilloscope: count the number of divisions for one complete cycle horizontally and multiply by the time-base value to get the period T. Frequency \(f = \frac{1}{T}\). For amplitude, count divisions from the centre to the peak and multiply by the Y-gain.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n36">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n37">Voltage is in volts (V), current in amperes (A) and resistance in ohms (&Omega;). Always include units in every answer.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n38">Series circuits: current is the same everywhere; voltages add to the supply. Parallel circuits: voltage is the same across every branch; currents add at junctions.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n39">A voltmeter connects in parallel; an ammeter in series. On an oscilloscope, amplitude is centre-to-peak (not peak-to-peak), and period is the time for one complete cycle.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n35">Oscilloscope reading questions are worth 3 marks and consistently lose students marks for unit confusion. Always write down the time-base value and the Y-gain value before calculating anything. Amplitude is measured from the <strong>centre line to the peak</strong> (NOT the full peak-to-peak distance). Period is the time for one <strong>complete cycle</strong>. If you calculate frequency, show \(f = 1/T\) explicitly &mdash; examiners award a mark for the correct method even if you make an arithmetic error. When asked to draw a circuit, use only standard symbols: a scruffy but correctly symbolised circuit beats a neat but non-standard one every time.</p>

