=== L5 Sensing Circuits and Voltage Dividers ===
id: 7cba0c7c-40ee-4493-ab15-a83f52533803
slug: sensing-circuits-and-voltage-dividers
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-15T21:15:42.372649+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 40

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Build voltage-divider sensing circuits using LDRs, thermistors and other input transducers.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">From Sensor to Signal</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">A sensor on its own produces a change in resistance, not a change in voltage. To turn a varying resistance into a varying voltage that a comparator or logic gate can read, you use a <dfn class="term" data-def="A circuit of two resistors in series across a supply, where the output voltage is taken from the junction between them. The output depends on the ratio of the two resistances.">voltage divider</dfn>. This is the most common input circuit in electronics and it appears on virtually every exam paper.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">How a Voltage Divider Works</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Two resistors, R1 and R2, are placed in series across a supply voltage \(V_{in}\). The output voltage \(V_{out}\) is taken from the junction between them and is given by:</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">$$V_{out} = V_{in} \times \frac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2}$$</p>
<p data-narration-id="n6">If R2 is much larger than R1, most of the supply voltage appears across R2, so \(V_{out}\) is high. If R2 is much smaller than R1, most of the voltage is dropped across R1 and \(V_{out}\) is low. Replacing one of the resistors with a sensor (LDR, thermistor, etc.) makes \(V_{out}\) change with the physical quantity being sensed.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n7" data-revision-tip="Cover this and write the voltage divider equation. Then state: if R2 increases, does V_out increase or decrease? Answer it without looking.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Voltage divider: \(V_{out} = V_{in} \times \dfrac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2}\). \(V_{out}\) is taken across R2. If R2 increases relative to R1, \(V_{out}\) increases. If R2 decreases, \(V_{out}\) decreases.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">LDR Sensing Circuit &mdash; Light Level Detection</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">A <dfn class="term" data-def="A resistor whose resistance decreases as the light intensity falling on it increases. Used in light-sensing voltage dividers.">light-dependent resistor (LDR)</dfn> has a high resistance in the dark (hundreds of kilohms) and a low resistance in bright light (hundreds of ohms). In a voltage divider with the LDR as R1 and a fixed resistor as R2:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n10"><strong>In the dark:</strong> LDR (R1) is large &rArr; most voltage drops across R1 &rArr; \(V_{out}\) is LOW.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n11"><strong>In bright light:</strong> LDR (R1) falls &rArr; less voltage drops across R1 &rArr; \(V_{out}\) rises towards \(V_{in}\).</li>
</ul>
<p data-narration-id="n12">Swapping the positions of the LDR and the fixed resistor (putting the LDR as R2 instead of R1) reverses the behaviour: \(V_{out}\) is HIGH in the dark and LOW in the light. The examiner has penalised this error repeatedly &mdash; always identify which resistor is connected to 0 V to determine what happens to \(V_{out}\).</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">NTC Thermistor Sensing Circuit &mdash; Temperature Detection</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">An <dfn class="term" data-def="A negative temperature coefficient thermistor: a resistor whose resistance decreases as temperature increases. Used in temperature-sensing voltage dividers.">NTC thermistor</dfn> works on the same principle. Its resistance falls as temperature rises. In a divider with the thermistor as R1:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n15"><strong>Cold:</strong> thermistor resistance is high &rArr; large voltage drop across thermistor &rArr; \(V_{out}\) is LOW.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n16"><strong>Hot:</strong> thermistor resistance falls &rArr; smaller voltage drop across thermistor &rArr; \(V_{out}\) rises.</li>
</ul>
<p data-narration-id="n17">If the circuit needs to trigger when it gets cold (e.g. a frost alarm), place the thermistor as R2 so \(V_{out}\) rises as temperature falls. This &ldquo;orientation&rdquo; decision is the main skill tested on exam questions about sensing circuits.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n18" data-revision-tip="Without looking: in a divider where the NTC thermistor is R1 (top, connected to supply) and a fixed resistor is R2 (bottom, connected to 0V), does V_out rise or fall when temperature increases? State why.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Sensor orientation determines output behaviour. Sensor as R1 (top): \(V_{out}\) rises when sensor resistance falls (e.g. hotter, brighter). Sensor as R2 (bottom): \(V_{out}\) falls when sensor resistance falls. Getting this wrong produces the opposite output to what is wanted.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n19">Other Sensing Types</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n20">The same voltage-divider principle applies to all resistive sensors in the specification:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n21"><strong>Pressure sensor (force-sensitive resistor):</strong> resistance decreases under applied force. Used in electronic door mats and scales.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22"><strong>Moisture sensor:</strong> two copper tracks on a PCB; resistance falls when water bridges the gap between them. Used in plant-watering monitors.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23"><strong>Sound sensor (electret microphone + amplifier):</strong> produces a small AC voltage proportional to sound level. The signal is amplified before being fed into a comparator.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24"><strong>Potentiometer (variable resistor):</strong> a manually-adjusted voltage divider. The wiper provides a continuously variable \(V_{out}\) between 0 V and \(V_{in}\).</li>
</ul>

<h2 data-narration-id="n25">Pulse Generator as an Input Sub-System</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n26">A <dfn class="term" data-def="An oscillator circuit that produces a continuous stream of regular pulses, used as a timing input signal for counters, timers and edge-triggered circuits.">pulse generator</dfn> produces a regular square wave. It is used as a timing input &mdash; for example, providing the clock pulses that advance a counter. The output of the pulse generator feeds the clock input of a processing circuit; it is never placed in the sensor position of a voltage divider.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n27">Pull-up and pull-down resistors are used with switches and other two-state inputs to ensure the logic level is well-defined when the switch is open. A pull-up resistor (connected between the input pin and the positive supply) holds the logic level HIGH when the switch is open; the switch itself pulls it LOW when closed. A pull-down resistor does the reverse.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n28">Sizing the LED Current-Limiting Resistor</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n29">When an LED is included in a sensing circuit output, a series resistor limits the current to a safe value. The LED has a typical forward voltage (given in the exam data: approximately 2 V for a red LED, 3 V for blue or white). The resistor takes the remaining voltage:</p>
<p data-narration-id="n30">$$R = \frac{V_{supply} - V_{LED}}{I_{LED}}$$</p>
<p data-narration-id="n31">For a 9 V supply, a 2 V red LED and a target current of 10 mA: \(R = (9 - 2)/0.010 = 700 \text{ }\Omega\). The nearest E24 value above 700 &Omega; is 750 &Omega; &mdash; always round up to keep current at or below the target.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Worked Example: Voltage Divider Output Calculation</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n32"><strong>Problem:</strong> A 9 V supply, a 10 k&Omega; fixed resistor (R1, top) and an LDR (R2, bottom). In daylight, the LDR resistance is 1 k&Omega;. Calculate \(V_{out}\).</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n33">\(V_{out} = V_{in} \times \frac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2} = 9 \times \frac{1000}{10000 + 1000} = 9 \times \frac{1000}{11000} = 9 \times 0.0909 = 0.82 \text{ V}\)</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n34">This LOW output voltage would not trigger a HIGH logic level. If the circuit is supposed to activate in daylight, the positions of R1 and R2 should be swapped.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Error: Getting Sensor Orientation Wrong</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n35">The most common mistake in sensing circuit questions is drawing the resistor and sensor in the wrong order. A question might ask for a circuit that triggers when it is warm. If you place the thermistor as R1 (top) and the fixed resistor as R2 (bottom), \(V_{out}\) rises when the thermistor gets hot &mdash; that&rsquo;s correct. If you accidentally swap them, \(V_{out}\) falls when it gets hot, which is the opposite of what is required.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n36">Always write a one-line logic check before drawing: &ldquo;Hot &rArr; thermistor resistance falls &rArr; if thermistor is R1, \(V_{out}\) rises &rArr; correct for triggering on warmth.&rdquo; This takes five seconds and prevents the most penalised error in sensing circuit questions.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n38">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n39">A voltage divider converts a varying resistance into a varying voltage: \(V_{out} = V_{in} \times R_2/(R_1 + R_2)\). Sensor orientation (top vs bottom) determines whether \(V_{out}\) rises or falls when the physical quantity increases.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n40">LDR resistance falls in bright light; NTC thermistor resistance falls when temperature rises. Place the sensor as R1 or R2 to get the output polarity your circuit needs.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n41">LED current-limiting resistor: \(R = (V_{supply} - V_{LED})/I_{LED}\). The LED forward voltage is provided in the exam; always round R up to the nearest E24 value.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p>Always round resistor values up to the nearest E24 value to ensure current does not exceed the LED&rsquo;s safe limit.</p>

