=== L6 Transistor and MOSFET Switching Circuits ===
id: 92ab1a18-181b-4e3f-8452-32b4f641526e
slug: transistor-and-mosfet-switching-circuits
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:41:45.227898+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 31

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Use npn transistors and n-channel MOSFETs as electronic switches, with calculations.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">Switching High Currents from Low-Power Signals</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">A logic gate or comparator output can drive only a few milliamperes &mdash; not nearly enough to light a lamp, spin a motor or click a relay. A transistor or MOSFET acts as a current amplifier and electronic switch, letting a small control signal turn a much larger load current on or off. Examiners report this is poorly understood every year, so this lesson builds the skill in both directions: forward (given a signal, will the device switch?) and backward (given a required load current, what control signal do I need?).</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">The npn Bipolar Transistor as a Switch</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">An <dfn class="term" data-def="A bipolar junction transistor with n-type collector and emitter and p-type base. A small base current controls a much larger collector current.">npn transistor</dfn> has three terminals: base (B), collector (C) and emitter (E). In a switching circuit:</p>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n5">The <strong>load</strong> (lamp, relay coil, etc.) is connected between the positive supply and the collector.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n6">The <strong>emitter</strong> connects to 0 V (ground).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n7">A <strong>base resistor</strong> connects the control signal to the base, limiting the base current to a safe value.</li>
</ul>
<p data-narration-id="n8">The transistor turns on (saturates) when a sufficient base current flows. The base-emitter voltage \(V_{BE}\) is approximately 0.7 V when the transistor is on. Any control voltage above about 0.7 V (after the base resistor voltage drop) will start to turn the transistor on. To ensure full saturation, the base current \(I_B\) must be at least \(I_C / h_{FE}\), where \(I_C\) is the required collector (load) current and \(h_{FE}\) is the transistor&rsquo;s DC current gain from its data sheet.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">$$I_B = \frac{I_C}{h_{FE}}$$</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n10" data-revision-tip="Cover this. Write down what V_BE equals when an npn transistor is on, and write the equation linking base current, collector current and current gain.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>npn transistor switch: \(V_{BE} \approx 0.7 \text{ V}\) when on. Current gain \(h_{FE} = I_C / I_B\). To saturate the transistor: \(I_B \geq I_C / h_{FE}\).</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Worked Example: Sizing the npn Base Resistor</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n11"><strong>Problem:</strong> A transistor with \(h_{FE}\) = 100 must switch a lamp drawing 200 mA. The control signal is 5 V. Find the minimum base current needed and a suitable base resistor value.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n12"><strong>Step 1 &mdash; minimum base current:</strong> \(I_B = I_C / h_{FE} = 200 / 100 = 2 \text{ mA} = 0.002 \text{ A}\)</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n13"><strong>Step 2 &mdash; voltage across base resistor:</strong> \(V_{RB} = V_{IN} - V_{BE} = 5 - 0.7 = 4.3 \text{ V}\)</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n14"><strong>Step 3 &mdash; base resistor:</strong> \(R_B = V_{RB} / I_B = 4.3 / 0.002 = 2150 \text{ }\Omega\). Nearest E24 value below 2150 &Omega; is 2.2 k&Omega; (rounding down keeps \(I_B\) at or above the minimum; 2.2 k&Omega; gives 4.3/2200 = 1.95 mA &mdash; just below; use 2 k&Omega; or accept 2.2 k&Omega; with a note that \(I_B\) is marginally insufficient; in practice, use \(R_B\) just below the calculated value). Check: is 1.95 mA sufficient? \(h_{FE} \times I_B = 100 \times 0.00195 = 195 \text{ mA}\) &mdash; very close to 200 mA. For safety, choose a base resistor of 1.8 k&Omega; to give a comfortable margin.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">The n-Channel Enhancement-Mode MOSFET</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16">An <dfn class="term" data-def="A voltage-controlled transistor that turns on when the gate-to-source voltage exceeds the threshold voltage. Gate current is effectively zero; used for high-current or high-speed switching.">n-channel MOSFET</dfn> has three terminals: gate (G), drain (D) and source (S). Unlike the bipolar transistor, the MOSFET is voltage-controlled: almost no gate current flows, so the control circuit does not need to supply base current. This makes MOSFETs ideal for driving high-power loads.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n17">The MOSFET turns on when the gate-to-source voltage \(V_{GS}\) exceeds the threshold voltage \(V_{GS(th)}\). For calculations, the threshold is taken as 3 V. When fully on (in the linear region), the drain current \(I_D\) is controlled by the transconductance:</p>
<p data-narration-id="n18">$$I_D = g_m (V_{GS} - V_{GS(th)})$$</p>
<p data-narration-id="n19">where \(g_m\) is the <dfn class="term" data-def="Transconductance: a measure of how effectively the gate voltage controls drain current in a MOSFET, in siemens (S) or amps per volt.">transconductance</dfn> in siemens (S or A/V). In a typical exam question \(g_m\) is given; you substitute \(V_{GS}\) and \(V_{GS(th)} = 3 \text{ V}\) to find \(I_D\).</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n20" data-revision-tip="Without looking: write the MOSFET drain current equation. State the assumed value of V_GS(th) used in calculations and explain why gate current is taken as zero.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>MOSFET: \(I_D = g_m(V_{GS} - V_{GS(th)})\). Threshold \(V_{GS(th)} = 3 \text{ V}\) (assumed). Gate current = 0. Turns on when \(V_{GS} > 3 \text{ V}\).</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Worked Example: MOSFET Drain Current Calculation</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n21"><strong>Problem:</strong> An n-channel MOSFET has \(g_m = 0.5 \text{ S}\) and \(V_{GS(th)} = 3 \text{ V}\). The gate voltage is 7 V. Calculate the drain current.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n22">\(I_D = g_m(V_{GS} - V_{GS(th)}) = 0.5 \times (7 - 3) = 0.5 \times 4 = 2 \text{ A}\)</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n23">This 2 A drain current could drive a motor. A bipolar transistor base circuit would need \(I_B = 2/h_{FE}\) &mdash; at \(h_{FE}\) = 100, that&rsquo;s 20 mA just to bias the base, which overloads a logic gate output. The MOSFET avoids this entirely because its gate draws negligible current.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n24">Choosing between npn and MOSFET</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n25">Use an npn bipolar transistor for low-to-medium current loads (up to around 100&ndash;200 mA) where the control circuit can supply a few milliamps of base current. Use an n-channel MOSFET for high-current loads (motors, solenoids) or where the control source (such as a logic gate output) cannot supply even a small base current. Both are placed between the processing sub-system and the output device as a transducer driver.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n26">Electromagnetic loads (relays, solenoids, motors) need a protection diode connected in <em>reverse bias</em> across the load. When the transistor or MOSFET switches off, the coil&rsquo;s collapsing magnetic field generates a large voltage spike (back-EMF) that can destroy the switch device. The diode clamps this spike. Note: a protection diode is only needed for electromagnetic loads, not for resistive loads like lamps.</p>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n28">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n29">npn transistor switch: \(V_{BE} \approx 0.7 \text{ V}\) when on; saturation needs \(I_B \geq I_C / h_{FE}\). Base resistor: \(R_B = (V_{IN} - 0.7) / I_B\).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n30">n-channel MOSFET: voltage-controlled (gate current = 0); turns on when \(V_{GS} > V_{GS(th)} = 3 \text{ V}\); drain current \(I_D = g_m(V_{GS} - V_{GS(th)})\).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n31">Use npn for low-power loads; use MOSFET for high-power loads or when the control source cannot supply base current. Electromagnetic loads always need a reverse-bias protection diode.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n27">The killer exam skill is working backwards: &ldquo;The lamp needs 300 mA; the transistor has \(h_{FE}\) = 150; the control signal is 5 V &mdash; is 5 V enough to saturate the transistor?&rdquo; Calculate \(I_B = 300/150 = 2 \text{ mA}\), then find the base resistor \(R_B = (5 - 0.7)/0.002 = 2150 \text{ }\Omega\). If the question then asks &ldquo;will the transistor switch on?&rdquo;, check whether the base current through the chosen resistor exceeds \(I_C / h_{FE}\). Always show \(V_{BE} = 0.7 \text{ V}\) explicitly &mdash; omitting it costs a mark. For MOSFET questions, state \(V_{GS(th)} = 3 \text{ V}\) and use the \(I_D = g_m(V_{GS} - V_{GS(th)})\) equation directly &mdash; gate current = 0 is an assumption to state, not derive.</p>

